1. tx_customers_junction.csv: customer master data (5,100 rows)
Column	Findings
customer_id	CUST00001–CUST05100, unique, no gaps
customer_postal_code	5-character strings with leading zeros (00990). There are 290 distinct codes, all starting 00/01/02, so it's all Helsinki/Espoo/Vantaa. Top codes: 00940, 01300, 02230
customer_age	18–90, median 44. Heavy peak in the 40s (2,096 people). 56 people are 80+
Edge cases:

Read postal code as a string. Read as a number, 00100 becomes 100.
Every file starts with a UTF-8 BOM (byte-order mark), so open them with utf-8-sig. Otherwise the first column name gets a hidden prefix.
Only 200 of the 5,100 customers have a mortgage. Their ages run 32–77 (median 47).
2. mtg_tx_customer_link_and_final_mtg_infio.csv: final outcome per mortgage (200 rows)
Mapping: maps C0004…C1000 to CUSTxxxxx one-to-one. Every ID resolves in the customer file.
Decisions: 150 approved and 50 rejected. The rejection reasons are insufficient_collateral (34), insufficient_income (8), insufficient_equity (6) and existing_debt (2).
Ranges:
Property price: €156k–538k
Loan: €121k–528k
Equity: €10k–81.5k
Loan-to-value (LTV): 0.76–0.98
Required income: €2.2k–9.7k per month
Formulas I confirmed:
loan + equity = price for all 200 rows.
lfinal_loan_to_value = loan / price exactly. The column name has a typo (lfinal_).
required_monthly_income = loan × 0.0184086 for every row. That is exactly a 6% stress rate, 25-year annuity, divided by a 35% debt-to-income cap. This is how the bank tests affordability.
Odd points:
The final collateral value is below the loan in 198 of 200 cases, and 150 of those are still approved. The other two have collateral exactly equal to the loan.
The final collateral value counts only the primary apartment's valuation. The second "other_apartment" valuation is ignored in 114 cases (see below).
There is no clean threshold between approved and rejected. Approved LTVs run 0.76–0.98 and rejected ones 0.79–0.97. "Insufficient equity" cases cluster at LTV ≥ 0.91 with equity of roughly €13k–17.5k.
3. mtg_negotiation_history.csv: event log (3,452 rows, 200 customers)
Time: 2 Jan 2026 – 10 Sep 2026. Timestamps use the SAS format 26MAY26:00:00:00, which parses with %d%b%y:%H:%M:%S. Only 5 events fall in September, so the data looks cut off there.

Size per customer: 11–25 events (median 17), spanning 13–45 days (median 25). Rows are already sorted by time within each customer, and no customer has two events at the same timestamp.

The process is the same for every application: application_created (always at 00:00:00) → contacts / property_added / price updates → loan_calculation → income_information_received → expense_recorded → collateral_added → collateral_valuation → additional_collateral_requested → a second valuation → document_received → loan_decision → often a follow-up customer_contact.

Properties: 1–3 per customer (113 / 58 / 29 customers). The decision is always on the last property added. Earlier properties are abandoned, which is why prices in the history reach €647.5k while the final maximum is €538k. Price updates move the price −6% to +8%, and 85 of the 188 updates are decreases.

Valuations: the bank values the primary apartment at 65–80% of the price (median 72%). The second "other_apartment" is valued at €51k–243k.

Expenses: six types, each with a fixed set of amounts:

Type	Range
Bank fee	€100–350
Valuation fee	€250–450
Maintenance fee	€180–550 per month
Transfer tax	€2.5k–18k
Renovation	€5k–30k
Existing loan	€0–1,200
Existing loan is often exactly €0. Only 37 customers have an existing loan above zero, and 30 of them were still approved. A customer can record the same expense type twice; C0004 has two transfer_tax entries.

Notes: free text, but generated from only 21 templates. Every number in them is already in the structured columns, so the notes add no information.

Edge cases in the history:

Columns are sparse, and which ones are filled depends on the event type. For example, customer_contact rows sometimes carry property, loan or decision values copied forward and sometimes nothing.
decision is filled on 336 rows, not 200, because the contact after a decision repeats it (136 rows). Use only loan_decision rows for the outcome.
The last event is not always the decision: 136 of the 200 histories end with a customer_contact.
collateral_status reads approved even for applications that were rejected.
78 customers had extra collateral requested but never got a second valuation. 8 approved customers had no extra collateral request at all.
The final table matches the history exactly: same decision, reason, loan, equity and last price, and its collateral equals the primary valuation on the loan_decision row.
4. tx_junction_transactions.csv: bank account transactions (3,626,755 rows, 343 MB, stored in Git LFS)
Columns: customer_id, bank_account_id, tx_date, tx_timestamp, category, tx_amount_eur, balance_after_eur, transaction_type, merchant_id, mcc_code

Size and performance: a single streaming pass in plain Python (csv module) takes about 2.7 minutes and very little memory. Don't load it into Excel. With pandas, read customer_id, bank_account_id, merchant_id and mcc_code as strings.

Coverage:
All 5,100 customers appear, each with exactly one account (ACC000001 = CUST00001, etc.). All 200 mortgage customers have transactions.
Dates run 1 Oct 2024 – 30 Sep 2026 (24 months), with an even ~150k transactions per month. The mortgage history (Jan–Sep 2026) falls inside this window, so behaviour before and after the mortgage application is visible.
148–1,543 transactions per account (median 700).
Timestamps use the same SAS format as the negotiation history (03OCT24:09:04:04), and tx_date always matches the timestamp's date. Activity runs 05:00–23:59, peaks around 16–18h and is highest on Fridays and Saturdays. Nothing happens between 00:00 and 05:00.

Data quality (better than expected):
No duplicate rows, no zero amounts, no unparseable timestamps.
Rows are sorted by account, then by time.
The balance chain is perfect: previous balance_after + tx_amount = balance_after on every row. You can therefore reconstruct the full daily balance history of any account.
Amounts have 1–2 decimals. The only empty fields are merchant_id and mcc_code (786,668 rows, all the same rows).

Categories and transaction types (category maps 1:1 to a type):
Type	Category	Rows	Sign	Amount range (EUR)
purchase	Groceries, Restaurants, Transportation, Entertainment, Health, Home & Tech, Lifestyle, Travel	3,000,711	always negative	−30,935 to −0.03 (mean −72)
utilities_payment	Utilities	342,728	negative	−1,098 to −2.58
salary	Salary	121,725	positive	356 to 22,583 (mean 3,362)
housing_payment	Housing	112,903	negative	−2,578 to −7.53 (mean −1,088)
investment_deposit	Invest Deposit	35,668	negative	−3,439 to −20
other_income	Other Income	6,299	positive	34 to 22,011
salary_sweep	Salary Sweep	6,189	negative	−9,200 to −314
investment_withdrawal	Invest Withdraw	532	positive	252 to 4,801
Outflows are only purchases, utilities, housing, investment deposits and salary sweeps. Inflows are only salary, other income and investment withdrawals. There are no transfers, refunds or card reversals.

Merchants (there are no free-text merchant descriptions; only IDs and MCC codes):
515 merchants, IDs like MRC-GRO-0035. The prefix encodes the category: GRO Groceries (80 merchants), RES Restaurants (90), TRN Transportation (65), LIF Lifestyle (65), HMT Home & Tech (60), HEA Health (55), ENT Entertainment (55), TRV Travel (45).
Each merchant always has exactly one category and one MCC code.
36 distinct MCC codes (standard card merchant category codes). Examples: 5411 grocery, 5812/5814 restaurants/fast food, 5542/5541 fuel, 4111 public transport, 4121 taxi, 7523 parking, 5912 pharmacy, 8011 doctors, 4511 airlines, 7011 hotels.
Very skewed popularity: MRC-GRO-0032 alone has 90,280 transactions, while the least used merchant has 92.
Edge case: 160,624 purchases have no merchant or MCC (mostly Transportation with 75k, then Restaurants, Health and Groceries). Non-purchase types never have a merchant.

Salary and income:
Every customer gets exactly one salary per month (a few months are missing, 121,725 of 122,400 possible). Most are paid on the 15th (96k); the rest at month end (26th–31st). Salary is stable per customer (median coefficient of variation 7.7%).
salary_sweep moves a large part of the salary out right after payday (on the 15th–17th or at month end), apparently into savings.
Key finding for the mortgage data: each mortgage customer's average monthly inflow (Jan 2025 – Aug 2026) is almost exactly equal to their required_monthly_income_eur (median ratio 1.00 for both approved and rejected; range 0.59–1.12). The loan amounts seem to have been sized to the customer's actual income, so income alone does not separate approved from rejected. Even the "insufficient_income" rejections have ratios of 0.98–1.05.

Balances:
Opening balances range from −1.41 to €19.8k (median €2.9k). Closing balances range from −€1.2k to €408k (median €6.2k). A few accounts accumulate very large balances (max €408k, CUST05034).
Overdrafts are common: 3,526 of 5,100 accounts go below zero at some point (lowest −€1,985). Most recover quickly (median 11 days negative over two years), but some stay negative for up to 216 days.

Spending behaviour:
Purchases take a median of 52% of a customer's inflow (10th–90th percentile 28%–69%). A few customers spend almost all their income.
Outliers: 104 single purchases of €5,000 or more (up to −€30,935, mostly Home & Tech and Travel). Some customers repeat them (CUST00448 has several).
Housing payments do not change after a mortgage decision. Only 78 of the 200 mortgage customers have housing payments both before and after their decision month, and for them the after/before ratio is 0.95–1.05. Approved mortgages do not show up as new loan payments in the account.

Edge cases:
Category and transaction_type are redundant; either one is enough.
Treat a missing merchant as "unknown merchant", not as bad data. Category is still filled.
Negative balances are normal (overdraft) and are not errors.
September 2026 is a complete month here, unlike the mortgage history which ends on 10 Sep.
Joining: tx customer_id → link file customer_id → mortgage_customer_id → negotiation history.